digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56765-2
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, the Haber–Bosch process (N2 + H2 -> NH3) is a major invention underlying modern society."];
  "N10" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Ru is ideal but scarce and scarcity implies the need for higher intrinsic activity, therefore the research goal should be to design higher-activity Ru catalysts."];
  "N11" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, technical Ru ammonia catalysts consist of supported Ru nanoparticles exposing multiple facets and sites."];
  "N12" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, Ru NP sizes/shapes are ill-defined, making it hard to disentangle which particles/sites control kinetics."];
  "N14" [label="(0,0,0) If catalyst nanoparticles have heterogeneous, ill-defined structures, then mapping observed kinetics to specific structures/sites becomes difficult, preventing clear structure–reactivity relations."];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given technical Ru NPs are heterogeneous/ill-defined and such heterogeneity hinders attributing kinetics to structure, therefore technical catalysts lack clear structure–reactivity understanding."];
  "N16" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, experiments often use well-defined Ru surfaces to understand the kinetic importance of different site types."];
  "N20" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, strategies focus on promoting undercoordinated sites or increasing the number of active sites on Ru nanoparticles."];
  "N23" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, ideal-surface model catalysts generally omit inherent strain, even though strain is always present in metal nanoparticles."];
  "N26" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, common surface models make it challenging to describe kinetic properties (TOF and apparent activation energy) of technical Ru nanoparticles."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) If real Ru nanoparticles have both multi-site complexity and inherent strain, then models that ignore site coupling and/or strain will likely mispredict TOF and apparent activation energy."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, the Haber–Bosch process requires harsh conditions (673–773 K and 10–30 MPa)."];
  "N31" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, experiments alone still struggle to uncover how kinetic coupling and inherent strain affect catalytic activity."];
  "N32" [label="(0,0,0) If experiments cannot isolate/quantify contributions of coupling and strain, then a mechanistic, site-resolved simulation approach is needed to separate and test these effects."];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) If one needs to capture multi-site reaction pathways, diffusion-mediated communication, and heterogeneous local environments (e.g., strain fields), then first-principles-based kMC is an appropriate modeling framework."];
  "N36" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, the combined effect of structural complexity and inherent strain is required to match experimentally measured TOF."];
  "N37" [label="(26,0,0) Currently, simulations indicate kinetic coupling boosts activity because N2 dissociation and NHx hydrogenation occur on different sites connected by rapid diffusion."];
  "N38" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, inherent random strain further enhances activity by promoting both N2 dissociation and NHx hydrogenation on their respective sites."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, ~2% of global energy consumption is attributed to Haber–Bosch, motivating catalysts active at lower temperatures."];
  "N40" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given mechanistic findings that coupling separates steps across sites, strain promotes key steps, and both are needed for TOF agreement, therefore the general design principle is to engineer Ru nanoparticles to combine site-assemblies (for kinetic coupling) with tailored strain distributions to improve NH3 synthesis and potentially circumvent scaling limits."];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given societal/energy need motivates low-T/P NH3 synthesis and Ru efficiency and a validated principle is that coupling+strain control TOF, therefore the research proposal is to use first-principles kMC-guided design of strain-engineered site-assemblies on Ru nanoparticles to create higher-activity Ru catalysts (breaking scaling constraints)."];
  "N42" [label="(0,0,0) If a chemical process is societally important and the product demand is high, then improving the process efficiency is a high-priority research target."];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) If a process efficiency improvement is a high-priority target and product demand is high, then there is strong practical motivation to pursue improved catalysts for that process."];
  "N47" [label="(0,0,0) If a process requires high temperature and high pressure, then it is typically energy intensive."];
  "N48" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Haber–Bosch requires harsh high-T/high-P conditions and such conditions imply high energy intensity, therefore Haber–Bosch is energy intensive, supporting the need for improved catalysts."];
  "N49" [label="(0,0,0) If a catalyst material is the ideal performer under industrial conditions, then it is a natural baseline for catalyst development efforts."];
  "N5" [label="(0,0,0) If a large-scale chemical process is energy intensive due to harsh operating conditions, then lowering operating temperature/pressure via better catalysts is a rational mitigation strategy."];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Ru is the ideal metal catalyst and ideal performers are natural baselines for development, therefore Ru should be the baseline material for developing improved NH3 synthesis catalysts."];
  "N54" [label="(0,0,0) If the development objective is higher-activity Ru catalysts, then design efforts should focus on mechanisms that increase TOF per Ru atom."];
  "N56" [label="(0,0,0) If technical catalysts expose multiple facets and sites and structure–reactivity relations are unclear, then identifying which sites control kinetics requires site-resolved modeling and/or characterization beyond idealized assumptions."];
  "N57" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given technical Ru catalysts expose multiple facets/sites and structure–reactivity relations are challenging and this implies a need for site-resolved approaches, therefore the research should adopt methods that resolve multiple coexisting site types."];
  "N58" [label="(0,0,0) If mechanistic understanding is mainly derived from well-defined single-crystal and mean-field models, then predictions may be limited when applied to heterogeneous nanoparticles."];
  "N59" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given studies use well-defined Ru surfaces/DFT/microkinetics and such approaches can be limited for heterogeneous nanoparticles, therefore difficulties in predicting technical NP kinetics are expected from these modeling assumptions."];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given Haber–Bosch is energy intensive and harsh and a general mitigation is lower-T/P via better catalysts, therefore research should target catalysts enabling lower-T/P NH3 synthesis."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) If dominant strategies emphasize increasing a single class of active sites (e.g., step/B5) on nanoparticles, then alternative multi-site mechanisms (e.g., kinetic coupling) may be underexplored."];
  "N61" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the prevailing view focuses on N2 dissociation at step sites and strategies emphasize undercoordinated/active-site counts and such focus may underexplore multi-site mechanisms, therefore investigating site assemblies and kinetic coupling is warranted."];
  "N62" [label="(0,0,0) If strain is always present in technical nanoparticles and strain modifies electronic structure and the reaction energy landscape, then ignoring strain in models can cause kinetic prediction errors."];
  "N63" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given strain is always present and strain modifies Ru d-states and the ammonia-synthesis PES and ignoring such strain can cause prediction errors, therefore strain must be considered to accurately model TOF and apparent activation energy."];
  "N65" [label="(0,0,0) If technical catalysts are nanoparticles exposing multiple facets/sites and technical nanoparticles are inherently strained, then technical Ru nanoparticles are both multi-site complex and inherently strained."];
  "N66" [label="(0,0,0) If characterization can measure strain distributions and synthesis can provide well-defined nanoparticles but experiments still cannot isolate kinetic coupling/strain effects, then simulations are needed to disentangle these contributions."];
  "N67" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given there is opportunity to link structure/function and strain can be measured but experiments still struggle to uncover coupling/strain effects and this implies simulations are needed, therefore a mechanistic simulation approach is necessary."];
  "N68" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, a site-resolved simulation is necessary to disentangle kinetic coupling and strain effects in Ru nanoparticles."];
  "N7" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, Ru is known as the ideal metal catalyst for industrial ammonia synthesis."];
  "N8" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, Ru is scarce; therefore higher-activity Ru catalysts are needed to use Ru efficiently and sustainably."];
  "N9" [label="(0,0,0) If the best-performing catalyst material is scarce, then improving its intrinsic activity (TOF per Ru) is necessary for sustainable deployment."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to develop and evaluate a first-principles kMC-guided framework for Ru nanoparticle ammonia-synthesis catalysts, using site-resolved kinetic coupling and inherent strain to motivate higher-activity Ru designs that could operate more efficiently under less demanding conditions.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across the motivation, modeling limitations, and mechanistic simulation findings, improved Ru nanoparticle ammonia-synthesis catalysts require linking site-resolved simulations of kinetic coupling and strain to TOF-oriented design principles."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because Ru is scarce, improving the intrinsic activity of Ru catalysts is necessary for efficient and sustainable use of Ru in ammonia synthesis."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Given the objective of improving intrinsic activity in Ru catalysts, Ru nanoparticle design should emphasize mechanisms that increase TOF per Ru atom."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Technical Ru nanoparticles can be treated as both multi-site structural ensembles and inherently strained nanoparticles."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Simulation evidence that structural complexity plus inherent strain is required to match measured TOF supports considering strain-engineered site assemblies as a promising Ru nanoparticle catalyst-design direction."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The difficulty of common surface models in describing TOF and apparent activation energy is plausibly explained by omitted multi-site coupling and/or strain effects in real Ru nanoparticles."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) First-principles-based kMC is an appropriate framework for studying kinetic coupling in Ru nanoparticle ammonia-synthesis kinetics when the relevant behavior involves multi-site reaction pathways connected by rapid diffusion."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because the Haber–Bosch process is societally important and ammonia demand is high, improving the efficiency of ammonia synthesis is a high-priority research target."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The energy burden of Haber–Bosch motivates targeting better catalysts that enable ammonia synthesis at lower temperature and/or pressure."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) High ammonia demand together with the high priority of improving Haber–Bosch efficiency provides practical motivation to pursue improved catalysts for ammonia synthesis."];
  "N5" -> "N6" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N4" -> "N6" [label="deduction-case"];
